Make $VIMRUNTIME resolve via argv[0] on Unix, with a stderr warning when the
resolved path is missing.

Upstream Linux/BSD vim never walks argv[0] to find $VIM/$VIMRUNTIME — it
relies entirely on the compile-time pathdef (set by --prefix) or the
'helpfile' option (which, in nixpkgs and other prefix-based builds, gets
pre-expanded to a `/nix/store/...` path that doesn't exist on the user's
machine). The exe-name code paths in misc1.c are dead code on Unix because:

  - feature.h gates USE_EXE_NAME to MSWIN/MACOS_X/VMS, so the macro isn't
    defined on plain Unix.
  - Even with USE_EXE_NAME, there's no Unix-side mch_get_exe_name() (only
    Windows has one), so the exe_name global stays NULL.
  - Even with exe_name populated, the helpfile branch is tried first, and
    when its walk lands on a non-existent path it falls through to the
    pathdef default instead of trying exe_name.
  - vim_version_dir only probes <vimdir>/<ver> and <vimdir>/runtime, never
    the FHS layout <vimdir>/../share/vim/<ver> that unpins ships.

This patch makes the relocatable layout work end-to-end:

1. feature.h: define USE_EXE_NAME on Unix.
2. os_unix.c: implement mch_get_exe_name() via readlink("/proc/self/exe").
3. proto/os_unix.pro: declare the new function so misc1.c can call it.
4. misc1.c::vim_getenv: call mch_get_exe_name() lazily on first invocation
   so exe_name is populated before any code path consumes it.
5. misc1.c::vim_getenv: after the helpfile-driven walk fails (VIM_CLEAR
   fires because the baked path doesn't exist on this machine), retry the
   walk with exe_name.
6. misc1.c::vim_version_dir: also probe <vimdir>/../share/vim/<ver>, so
   exe at <root>/bin/<exe> can find runtime at <root>/share/vim/<ver>/.
7. misc1.c::vim_getenv: emit a one-shot stderr warning if the resolved
   $VIMRUNTIME doesn't pass mch_isdir — turns a silent
   "menus/syntax/help missing" deploy bug into an actionable message.

--- a/src/feature.h
